  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 239 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nGEFS and EC 500-mb height mean-spread guidance is supportive of\na longwave upper-level ridge centered across the High Plains \nfor the entirety of the long term period. The feature appears \nstrong enough that shortwave troughs from the Aleutian Islands \nwill be redirected northeastward before they encroach into the \nUnited States. However, broad surface lows in association with \nthese troughs look to favor southerly surface flow across the \nforecast region at least through Sunday night. Hot conditions \nappear favored over the weekend, with highs forecast in the mid \n90s to lower 100s Saturday, and upper 90s to mid 100s Sunday. \nHeat index values in the mid 100s are possible, especially \nSunday along and east of Kansas Highway 25 where dew points in \nthe upper 50s to lower 60s are possible. NBM guidance suggests \nover a 90% chance for much of Northwest Kansas and Southwest \nNebraska to experience temperatures over 100 degrees Sunday \nafternoon. Confidence in a Heat Advisory being needed Sunday is \naround 30%. In addition to the hot weather, dry conditions are \nforecast as well. Relative humidities (RH) are forecast to be \nlow, especially on Sunday when they drop into the upper teens \nacross portions of Eastern Colorado. Even so, critical fire \nweather conditions are not yet a concern, as wind gusts are \nforecast to max out in the low to mid 20 mph. NBM guidance \nsuggests a 20% chance or less across Eastern Colorado that wind \ngusts will meet critical fire weather criteria (25 mph or \ngreater). As such, confidence in a Red Flag Warning being needed\nacross this zone is less than 5%.\n\nGEFS 850-mb height mean-spread guidance shows a stationary low \ndeveloping across portions of Utah and Colorado Monday. This may\nallow for showers and thunderstorms to return to the area \nMonday through Wednesday. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance shows\nthe highest chances to receive greater than 0.1 inches of \nprecipitation across Eastern Colorado, with some areas over a \n40% chance each day. Thunderstorms are not favored to become \nsevere during this period, as GFS and EC model soundings show \n0-6km shear maxing out around 30 kts. However, it may be \nimportant to note that GFS soundings are showing a deep \ninverted-V profile up to nearly 500-mb. If thunderstorms could \noccur with this sounding profile, microbursts would be possible,\nwith a chance of experiencing severe winds. Confidence in \nsevere weather occurring is about 5% for each day Monday through\nWednesday. Temperatures in the mid 100s may continue into \nMonday as well. NBM guidance suggests between a 70-90% chance \nfor temperatures to rise above 100 degrees. Confidence in a Heat\nAdvisory being needed Monday is around 15%. High temperatures \nbegin slightly dropping on Tuesday into the mid 90s to lower \n100s, and low to mid 90s Wednesday, which would dramatically \ndecrease the likelihood of further Heat Advisories.",
